  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and lower humidity continue \nearly this week. There is an elevated rip current risk at area \nbeaches today and especially Labor Day.\n\nAfternoon wx analysis shows strong low pressure near Nova Scotia \nwith high pressure over the Great Lakes building in toward the area. \nOnshore flow has allowed for the pleasant wx this afternoon, with \ntemps around 80F and dew pts dropping into the upper 50s-mid 60s. \nShowers across far SW portions of the FA should exit in the next few \nhours, giving way to dry conditions tonight. Low temperatures Monday \nmorning are near normal (upper 50s-lower 60s), but is much cooler \nthan it has been given the heat we experienced late last week. \nOnshore flow continues on Labor Day with low-mid 80s, continued \nlower humidity, and mostly dry wx. There is a very slight chance of \na shower near the coast throughout the day. East-northeast winds may \nbecome a bit breezy towards the coast, especially Hampton Roads and \nsouth into the NE NC beaches. Note that the rip current risk will \nlikely be significant tomorrow as onshore winds increase and \nnearshore waves build. A bit cooler Mon night as the high settles \noverhead. Widespread upper 50s are expected inland, with localized \nmid 50s possible. The wind lightens up on Tue as the high gradually \npushes offshore by late in the day. Warmer but not too humid with \nmid to locally upper 80s expected.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot weather returns by Wednesday/Thursday. There is \na chance for thunderstorms late this week as another cold front \napproaches. Slightly cooler temperatures return next weekend.\n\nBy Wednesday, the surface high slides well offshore as strong low \npressure tracks across Ontario and the associated cold front drops \ninto the Mississippi River Valley. Here locally, the repositioning \nof the ridge over the southern Mid-Atlantic and Southeast will allow \ntemperatures to warm up a few degrees as highs top out in the upper \n80s to around 90 degrees. Dewpoints will start to creep back up as \nwell, but will remain well below the oppressive levels we saw at the \nend of last week. The cold front continues to approach on Thursday, \nand will be near our northern CWA border by Thursday evening. \nThursday will be the hottest day of the week, with low to locally \nmid 90s possible. Dry conditions continue into the start of \nThursday, but shower/tstm chances increase north of I-64 Thursday \naftn/evening. Diurnal weakening occurs Thursday night, with the best \nshower/storm chances shifting to southern portions of the area on \nFriday. Not expecting widespread severe wx though could see an \nisolated strong to severe storm (mainly on Thursday). Temps drop \nback to around average by Saturday behind the front.",
